                    I'm a dermatologist. My colleagues and                      
                    I have curated a skin lesion dataset                        
                    over the past several years. I need a                       
                    model trained on this data to classify                      
                    lesions as malignant or benign. The                         
                    trained model should be saved, and I                        
                    need a function that takes a folder                         
                    path of new images and returns a                            
                    malignancy probability (0–1) for each                       
                    image. Be fair towards skin tone.                           
                                                                                
                    ## Task evaluation                                          
                                                                                
                    Area under the receiver operator curve                      
                    (AUROC)                                                     
                                                                                
                    # Memory                                                    
                                                                                
                    Design: I propose fine-tuning a                             
                    pretrained ResNet18 on the binary                           
                    malignant vs non-malignant                                  
                    (benign+non-neoplastic) task using                          
                    PyTorch. Images and labels are loaded                       
                    via a custom Dataset from the provided                      
                    CSV and image folder, with standard                         
                    augmentations for training and                              
                    normalization. We perform an 80/20                          
                    train/validation split and train for                        
                    several epochs with cross-entropy loss                      
                    and Adam optimizer. Evaluation is done                      
                    via ROC AUC on the hold-out set, and we                     
                    provide a predict() function that                           
                    returns malignancy probabilities for                        
                    new images in a folder. The trained                         
                    model weights are saved to                                  
                    `working/model.pth`.                                        
                    Results: The training script executed                       
                    successfully without any errors. The                        
                    model was trained for 5 epochs with                         
                    decreasing loss values, indicating                          
                    effective learning. The validation ROC                      
                    AUC of 0.8940 demonstrates strong                           
                    classification performance for                              
                    malignancy detection on the skin lesion                     
                    dataset.                                                    
                    Validation Metric: 0.894                                    
                                                                                
                    -------------------------------                             
                    Design: I propose fine-tuning a                             
                    pretrained EfficientNet-B0 from the                         
                    timm library to classify malignant                          
                    versus non-malignant lesions by                             
                    replacing its head with a single output                     
                    for malignancy probability. We’ll use                       
                    standard augmentations including random                     
                    resized crop and horizontal flips,                          
                    normalize images, and perform an 80/20                      
                    stratified split for training and                           
                    validation. Training will use                               
                    BCEWithLogitsLoss and the Adam                              
                    optimizer for 5 epochs, evaluating                          
                    performance via ROC AUC on the                              
                    validation set. A reusable predict()                        
                    function will load the saved model and                      
                    output malignancy probabilities for any                     
                    folder of images.                                           
                    Results: The training script ran                            
                    successfully without any errors. The                        
                    model was trained for 5 epochs using                        
                    EfficientNet-B0 architecture with                           
                    pretrained weights, and the validation                      
                    ROC AUC achieved was approximately                          
                    0.896, indicating good discriminative                       
                    performance for malignancy                                  
                    classification. The model was saved                         
                    correctly for future inference, and the                     
                    provided prediction function is                             
                    well-structured to output malignancy                        
                    probabilities for new images.                               
                    Validation Metric: 0.8959                                   
                                                                                
